  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1250 AM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nWe continue to closely monitor a weekend weather system, expected\nto bring cooler temperatures and chances for rain/thunderstorms. \nThe initially coherent upper wave across the Great Basin \ncontinues to trend towards becoming \"strung out\"/sheared with time\ninto Saturday. \n\nThe more interesting model trend over the past 24 hours has been \nthe emergent depiction of a trailing vorticity maxima rounding the\nbase of the broader trough across the Four Corners into the \nOklahoma/Texas Panhandles by Saturday evening. With a more direct \nshot of synoptic forcing, and more notable/stronger surface \nreflection, chances for rain & storms are back on the rise on \nSaturday and Saturday night, both ahead of and along/near the \napproaching surface front. \n\nIn terms of impact(s) brought by this activity, lightning looks to  \nbe the primary concern, with very limited instability (due to \nmeager moisture return & warm mid-level temperatures) precluding \na risk for strong-severe storms. \"Most likely\" rainfall totals \n(up to a few tenths) also don't point towards much in the way of \nhydrologic concern as of now.\n\nAs a surface low rapidly advances northeastward into the Central \nPlains, a cold front will begin to shove southeastward across the \narea. The modified post-frontal airmass still looks to be \nrelatively modest (i.e., less cold) by late November standards, \nthough a cooldown by upwards of 10-15 degrees remains likely into \nSunday across the area.\n\nAnother storm system looks poised to impact the region on Monday \ninto Tuesday, bringing additional chances for rain/storms and a \nreinforcement to cooler temperatures.\n\nUngar",
